<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union of Kėdainiai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1655">1655</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Union of Kėdainiai was an agreement signed in October 1655 at Kėdainiai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-radziwill">Janusz Radziwiłł</a> and other Lithuanian nobles with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. During the Russian and Swedish invasions known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>, it ended the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> under Swedish protection. It never took effect and collapsed within months.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-of-kedainiai.jpg" alt="Union of Kėdainiai" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kedainiai_Union.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-radziwill">Janusz Radziwiłł</a>, head of the Biržai line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radziwill-family">Radziwiłł family</a> and a leader of Lithuanian Protestants, was an opponent of King John Casimir and had already sought contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> to separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from the Crown. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> fell to the Russians on 3 October 1654, he entered secret talks with Sweden. Swedish troops crossed into the Commonwealth in July 1655, and once the king ordered the Crown army out of Lithuania, Janusz, Bishop Jerzy Tyszkiewicz of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> and Bogusław Radziwiłł called on the Swedes for help on 29 July. Russian forces took Vilnius on 9 August.</p><p>On 17 August Janusz signed the Treaty of Kėdainiai, which placed the Grand Duchy under Swedish protection. The nobility around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> rejected it, and at Virbalis on 23 August soldiers and nobles led by Paweł Sapieha formed a confederation loyal to John II Casimir. The Swedes did not approve the August act, and talks on a new treaty began in September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The agreement of 20 October, signed at Radziwiłł's castle by over 1,000 members of the Lithuanian nobility, declared the Polish–Lithuanian union null and void. In exchange for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the Grand Duchy was to be a Swedish protectorate joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in personal union, ruled by a royal governor, first Magnus Gabriel De la Gardie and later Bengt Skytte, with three deputies from each powiat. The armies were to be united, and the nobility lost free election and its say over war and peace but kept its other rights and religious freedoms. The Radziwiłłs were to receive two sovereign principalities from their lands, and Janusz secured large territorial grants and revenues in a private agreement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-radziwill">Janusz Radziwiłł</a> died on 31 December at Tykocin Castle, besieged by forces loyal to John II Casimir; Paweł Jan Sapieha took the castle and succeeded him as Grand Hetman. A popular uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> broke Swedish power, and the occupation provoked a similar rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Bogusław lost his army at the Battle of Prostki and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> in 1669. Contemporaries called Janusz Zdrajca, traitor; some modern historians see in his choice Realpolitik or a search for an ally against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, Lithuanian intellectuals of the National Revival such as Maironis praised the attempt to secede, and in 1995 Lithuania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> marked the union's 340th anniversary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Union of Kėdainiai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
